# circuitcenter.ai — Postfix virtual alias map
#
# Installed to /tmp/docker-mailserver/postfix-virtual.cf inside the container
# (the `mail-config` / `dms-config` volume) by ./setup-mailboxes.sh.
# DMS copies it verbatim to /etc/postfix/virtual on every change-detection pass.
#
# Format: <alias>  <recipient>   (whitespace separated, one per line)
#
# ===========================================================================
# ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. READ ./aliases.md BEFORE EDITING.
#
# Postfix resolves `user@domain` by trying, in order: `user@domain`, then
# `@domain`. A bare `@domain` wildcard therefore matches EVERY address in the
# domain that has no more specific entry — including real mailboxes. Without
# the self-alias block below, mail to anthony@circuitcenter.ai would be
# rewritten to no-reply@circuitcenter.ai and Anthony's mailbox would sit empty
# while nobody sees an error anywhere.
#
# So: every real account gets an identity entry, and the wildcard goes LAST.
# ===========================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Self-aliases — one per real mailbox. These EXIST ONLY to out-rank the
#    wildcard at the bottom of this file. They are not forwards.
#
#    ADD A LINE HERE WHENEVER YOU ADD A MAILBOX. A new account without a
#    self-alias silently loses all of its mail to the catch-all.
#
#    `setup alias add` cannot create these — it refuses any alias whose name
#    matches an existing account — so this file is edited by hand (upstream's
#    documented workaround). setup-mailboxes.sh verifies every account it
#    manages has a line here and refuses to install the file otherwise.
#
#    Expected, harmless startup log line, one per entry:
#      "Alias '<addr>' will not be added to '/etc/dovecot/userdb' twice"
#    DMS generates an identical userdb row and de-duplicates it. Nothing to fix.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

anthony@circuitcenter.ai    anthony@circuitcenter.ai
daniel@circuitcenter.ai     daniel@circuitcenter.ai
matthew@circuitcenter.ai    matthew@circuitcenter.ai
ronald@circuitcenter.ai     ronald@circuitcenter.ai
no-reply@circuitcenter.ai   no-reply@circuitcenter.ai

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Friendly public addresses -> the shared company inbox.
#
#    no-reply@ is the shared role account P4a ingests over IMAP. Publish these
#    on the site instead of no-reply@ — "no-reply" reads as send-only and
#    discourages the replies this inbox exists to collect. Same mailbox either
#    way.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

hello@circuitcenter.ai      no-reply@circuitcenter.ai
contact@circuitcenter.ai    no-reply@circuitcenter.ai
info@circuitcenter.ai       no-reply@circuitcenter.ai
sales@circuitcenter.ai      no-reply@circuitcenter.ai
support@circuitcenter.ai    no-reply@circuitcenter.ai

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Role addresses required (or expected) by RFC 2142 / RFC 5321.
#    The wildcard would cover these anyway; they are explicit so they keep
#    working if the wildcard is ever removed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

postmaster@circuitcenter.ai no-reply@circuitcenter.ai
abuse@circuitcenter.ai      no-reply@circuitcenter.ai
# Destination for DMARC aggregate reports (DNS `rua=mailto:` target).
dmarc@circuitcenter.ai      no-reply@circuitcenter.ai

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Catch-all — MUST BE THE LAST ENTRY IN THIS FILE.
#
#    Anything not matched above lands in the shared inbox rather than bouncing,
#    so a typo'd address or an address printed on old collateral still reaches
#    a human. Trade-offs and how to turn this off: see ./aliases.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@circuitcenter.ai           no-reply@circuitcenter.ai
